1
00:00:01,697 --> 00:00:03,699
[theme music]

2
00:00:03,786 --> 00:00:05,483
[Lisbon]
'You're gonna like this one.
It's unusual.'

3
00:00:05,570 --> 00:00:07,529
'You are never gonna guess
how the victim was found.'

4
00:00:07,616 --> 00:00:08,791
Oh, really?

5
00:00:08,878 --> 00:00:10,836
[animal roars]

6
00:00:10,923 --> 00:00:13,143
That is unusual.

7
00:00:13,230 --> 00:00:15,972
Panthera tigris,
if I'm not mistaken.

8
00:00:16,059 --> 00:00:19,671
And there's soft loamy soil
around here

9
00:00:19,758 --> 00:00:21,499
easily worked.

10
00:00:21,586 --> 00:00:23,458
Ergo, a hungry tiger
dug up the body.

11
00:00:23,545 --> 00:00:26,635
Not fair. Uh, there's
a big cat sanctuary around here.

12
00:00:26,722 --> 00:00:29,333
A drunk guy broke in,
freed the tigers

13
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:31,466
including the one
that discovered our victim.

14
00:00:31,553 --> 00:00:33,033
From the general
state of calm, I take it

15
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:34,860
that all the tigers
have been rounded up?

16
00:00:34,947 --> 00:00:36,514
As far as you know.

17
00:00:36,601 --> 00:00:38,386
[tigers growling]

18
00:00:38,473 --> 00:00:39,909
[Patrick]
'Hello, kitty-cat.'

19
00:00:39,996 --> 00:00:42,738
[growling continues]

20
00:00:42,825 --> 00:00:44,522
Hard to believe
it's a man-eater.

21
00:00:44,609 --> 00:00:47,047
It was still clawing up
the ground when they got here.

22
00:00:47,134 --> 00:00:48,178
Come on.

23
00:00:48,265 --> 00:00:49,832
[growling continues]

24
00:00:49,919 --> 00:00:52,661
The coroner's investigator
thinks it's suffocation.

25
00:00:52,748 --> 00:00:54,271
Looks like the victim
was killed somewhere else

26
00:00:54,358 --> 00:00:56,578
and then buried here,
based on the lividity pattern.

27
00:00:56,665 --> 00:00:58,536
Oh. Plus there's
a wheelbarrow track here.

28
00:00:58,623 --> 00:00:59,798
Oh, yeah.
Yeah, that, too.

29
00:01:01,844 --> 00:01:03,019
Who was he?

30
00:01:03,106 --> 00:01:04,803
John Doe.
No wallet, no ID.

31
00:01:04,890 --> 00:01:06,240
Well, don't fret.

32
00:01:06,327 --> 00:01:08,633
Rich dead men don't go
unclaimed for very long.

33
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:10,026
Rich? He's wearing hemp.

34
00:01:10,113 --> 00:01:12,159
He certainly is.
And who wears hemp?

35
00:01:12,246 --> 00:01:15,771
Other than rich men
and medieval peasants?

36
00:01:15,858 --> 00:01:17,381
Yeah, this one's
been reupholstered.

37
00:01:17,468 --> 00:01:19,166
Check out
that tight jawline.

38
00:01:19,253 --> 00:01:21,298
He's certainly not
a medieval peasant.

39
00:01:21,385 --> 00:01:22,299
Face-lift?

40
00:01:22,386 --> 00:01:23,735
I'm guessing.

41
00:01:23,822 --> 00:01:26,129
'Oh. It's a
black diamond wedding band.'

42
00:01:26,216 --> 00:01:28,131
'That's a current trend.
Am I right?'

43
00:01:28,218 --> 00:01:30,307
Looks like he was dieting.
Check out his belt.

44
00:01:30,394 --> 00:01:32,266
[Patrick]
'Yeah. Rapid weight loss.'

45
00:01:32,353 --> 00:01:35,791
Could be dieting.
Could be something else.

46
00:01:35,878 --> 00:01:37,053
[inhales deeply]
Ew!

47
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:39,011
That's not decomp.
What is that?

48
00:01:39,099 --> 00:01:41,623
[exhales]
The smell of desperation?

49
00:01:41,710 --> 00:01:43,538
Got a match
on our John Doe.

50
00:01:43,625 --> 00:01:45,496
Archer Braddock.
Local car dealer.

51
00:01:45,583 --> 00:01:47,150
Wife and two children.

52
00:01:47,237 --> 00:01:49,065
His wife filed
a missing persons last night.

53
00:01:49,152 --> 00:01:52,329
It says Braddock
had stage 4 bone cancer.

54
00:01:52,416 --> 00:01:54,723
Killing a man
that's already dying.

55
00:01:54,810 --> 00:01:56,551
That's a little redundant,
don't you think?

56
00:01:56,638 --> 00:01:58,292
[man on radio]
'Be advised that
one tiger is still outstanding.'

57
00:01:58,379 --> 00:01:59,858
- 'They made a mistake..'
- What did he say?

58
00:01:59,945 --> 00:02:01,425
...counting the tigers.
There's still one on the loose!

59
00:02:01,512 --> 00:02:03,297
We'll meet you back
at the CBI.

60
00:02:03,384 --> 00:02:04,341
[indistinct chatter]

61
00:02:04,428 --> 00:02:05,603
You are a cool customer.

62
00:02:05,690 --> 00:02:07,127
You think you can
outrun a tiger?

63
00:02:07,214 --> 00:02:09,781
I don't have to. I just have
to run faster than you.

64
00:02:09,868 --> 00:02:11,740
[instrumental music]

65
00:02:13,394 --> 00:02:15,265
[theme music]

66
00:02:18,181 --> 00:02:19,748
[music continues]

67
00:02:23,186 --> 00:02:24,492
[instrumental music]

68
00:02:24,579 --> 00:02:26,972
[baby crying]

69
00:02:30,411 --> 00:02:33,283
[crying continues]

70
00:02:36,373 --> 00:02:37,418
[female #1]
'It's okay, honey.'

71
00:02:37,505 --> 00:02:40,290
[crying continues]

72
00:02:40,377 --> 00:02:43,685
Sorry.
Excuse me.

73
00:02:43,772 --> 00:02:45,556
You ready to be a daddy?
That's gonna be you soon.

74
00:02:45,643 --> 00:02:47,384
Oh no. I can cry
much louder than that.

75
00:02:52,955 --> 00:02:54,261
Where's Cho?

76
00:02:54,348 --> 00:02:55,305
I haven't seen him yet.

77
00:02:55,392 --> 00:02:57,133
Coroner investigator's report.

78
00:02:57,220 --> 00:02:59,701
Putting the TOD between eight
at night and two in morning.

79
00:02:59,788 --> 00:03:01,093
That's a pretty big window.

80
00:03:01,181 --> 00:03:02,704
What did the coroner say
about cause of death?

81
00:03:02,791 --> 00:03:04,227
Uh, she didn't, she's dealing
with a multi-victim

82
00:03:04,314 --> 00:03:05,881
pile-up on the I-80.

83
00:03:05,968 --> 00:03:08,666
No coroner. Alright,
what about motive, suspects?

84
00:03:08,753 --> 00:03:11,191
Braddock had a lot of money.
He had to have had enemies.

85
00:03:11,278 --> 00:03:13,236
Braddock owned
three luxury car dealerships

86
00:03:13,323 --> 00:03:14,672
but when he found out
he was dying

87
00:03:14,759 --> 00:03:16,587
he sold his stake to his
employees at a discount.

88
00:03:16,674 --> 00:03:17,893
The man was kind of a saint.

89
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:19,503
Do we have any leads?

90
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:21,157
Braddock wascarrying
a pocket calendar.

91
00:03:21,244 --> 00:03:22,506
His last appointment
before his murder

92
00:03:22,593 --> 00:03:24,465
was at 4:17 p.m.

93
00:03:24,552 --> 00:03:26,902
Pretty specific time.

94
00:03:26,989 --> 00:03:28,295
The entry just says
"Treatment."

95
00:03:28,382 --> 00:03:29,992
- Doctor's treatment?
- I don't know.

96
00:03:30,079 --> 00:03:32,037
And a lot of entries like that
over the last few weeks

97
00:03:32,124 --> 00:03:33,430
each one at a weird time.

98
00:03:33,517 --> 00:03:34,953
I'll go and talk
to the wife about it.

99
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:36,346
You two stay here
and keep pushing.

100
00:03:36,433 --> 00:03:38,609
And tellCho to
get his ass to work.

101
00:03:38,696 --> 00:03:41,569
[instrumental music]

102
00:03:44,963 --> 00:03:46,878
[snoring]

103
00:03:50,882 --> 00:03:53,233
[music continues]

104
00:03:56,323 --> 00:03:58,238
[music continues]

105
00:04:00,370 --> 00:04:01,328
Good morning, Kimball Cho.

106
00:04:01,415 --> 00:04:02,764
- Good morning.
- Hmm?

107
00:04:02,851 --> 00:04:04,156
That smells good.

108
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:06,376
It's hand-pressed French roast.

109
00:04:06,463 --> 00:04:08,073
Mmm.

110
00:04:09,249 --> 00:04:10,380
[cap pops]

111
00:04:10,467 --> 00:04:12,991
[pills rattle]

112
00:04:13,078 --> 00:04:14,863
Hey, those are mine.

113
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:16,691
It's for pain.

114
00:04:16,778 --> 00:04:19,389
I've got pain.

115
00:04:19,476 --> 00:04:21,043
- No, you don't.
- Mmm.

116
00:04:22,697 --> 00:04:24,133
Don't be so stingy.

117
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:26,222
I'm not stingywith you, am I?

118
00:04:26,309 --> 00:04:28,093
No.

119
00:04:28,180 --> 00:04:29,312
No, you're not.

120
00:04:31,836 --> 00:04:35,405
Mmm!

121
00:04:35,492 --> 00:04:37,538
[instrumental music]

122
00:04:39,279 --> 00:04:41,455
[cell phone rings]
Um-uh.

123
00:04:41,542 --> 00:04:42,847
[phone ringing continues]
No.

124
00:04:42,934 --> 00:04:44,632
No, no, no, no, no.
Not-no, not now.

125
00:04:44,719 --> 00:04:46,503
- It might be work.
- Oh-ah.

126
00:04:46,590 --> 00:04:47,722
[ringing continues]

127
00:04:50,246 --> 00:04:51,421
Ah, that can't be right.

128
00:04:53,641 --> 00:04:55,469
My alarm didn't go off.

129
00:04:55,556 --> 00:04:58,123
Oh. That's because
I turned your alarm off.

130
00:04:58,210 --> 00:05:00,430
I thought you could use
some more sleep.

131
00:05:00,517 --> 00:05:02,432
[sighs]
Damn it, Summer,
I'm late for work.

132
00:05:02,519 --> 00:05:04,521
Lisbon's gonna be
somad at you.

133
00:05:04,608 --> 00:05:05,914
Scary.

134
00:05:06,001 --> 00:05:07,829
No. No. I'm never
late for work.

135
00:05:09,526 --> 00:05:10,701
Well, maybe
it's a good thing.

136
00:05:10,788 --> 00:05:12,877
I mean, it's a...new experience.

137
00:05:14,662 --> 00:05:16,707
You know?

138
00:05:16,794 --> 00:05:17,795
I gotta get ready.

139
00:05:21,712 --> 00:05:24,672
[car approaching]

140
00:05:24,759 --> 00:05:27,152
[Lisbon]
'Looks like somebody's
in for a big inheritance.'

141
00:05:27,239 --> 00:05:29,329
Mm, I suppose, if you go
for that sort of thing.

142
00:05:30,155 --> 00:05:31,113
You mean money?

143
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:33,245
I'll take health any day.

144
00:05:33,333 --> 00:05:34,812
Once the game's over,
the king and the pawn

145
00:05:34,899 --> 00:05:36,945
go back into the same box.

146
00:05:37,032 --> 00:05:39,687
[doorbell rings]
Shall we?

147
00:05:39,774 --> 00:05:41,210
We're with the CBI.

148
00:05:41,297 --> 00:05:43,517
I'm Agent Teresa Lisbon.
This is Patrick Jane.

149
00:05:43,604 --> 00:05:45,519
- Uh, can we come in?
- Yeah.

150
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:51,568
[door clicks]

151
00:05:53,091 --> 00:05:54,136
Uh..

152
00:05:54,223 --> 00:05:55,703
Are you Liesl?

153
00:05:55,790 --> 00:05:56,791
That's right.

154
00:05:56,878 --> 00:05:58,532
We're sorry for your loss.

155
00:05:58,619 --> 00:06:00,534
I don't...want to talk
about it.

156
00:06:00,621 --> 00:06:02,927
Of course you don't...uh, maybe
you can TO us

157
00:06:03,014 --> 00:06:04,189
to someone in charge.

158
00:06:05,234 --> 00:06:06,844
You sound like my dad.

159
00:06:06,931 --> 00:06:08,585
- TO?
- Car sales lingo.

160
00:06:08,672 --> 00:06:10,500
- Who manages the house?
- Helen.

161
00:06:10,587 --> 00:06:11,501
Your mother?

162
00:06:11,588 --> 00:06:13,329
Stepmother, I'm guessing.

163
00:06:13,416 --> 00:06:15,070
She's upstairs...in bed.

164
00:06:15,157 --> 00:06:17,420
Could you ask her to come down
and talk to us?

165
00:06:17,507 --> 00:06:20,510
She can't handle
the stairs right now.

166
00:06:20,597 --> 00:06:22,077
She's too weak.

167
00:06:23,165 --> 00:06:25,689
Helen has cancer, too.

168
00:06:25,776 --> 00:06:28,344
Welcome to the house of fun.

169
00:06:32,130 --> 00:06:33,567
That's how I met Archer.

170
00:06:33,654 --> 00:06:36,178
We were attending
a cancer support group.

171
00:06:36,265 --> 00:06:37,919
How long ago was that?

172
00:06:38,006 --> 00:06:40,051
Nine months ago.

173
00:06:40,138 --> 00:06:42,402
I know it sounds
a little...strange

174
00:06:42,489 --> 00:06:44,708
but it was quite
a romantic courtship.

175
00:06:44,795 --> 00:06:47,015
And a short one.

176
00:06:47,102 --> 00:06:48,669
Well, that's what happens
when you're living

177
00:06:48,756 --> 00:06:51,193
with an expiration date.

178
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:53,325
The wedding was two months ago.

179
00:06:53,413 --> 00:06:55,632
Liesl was a bridesmaid.

180
00:06:55,719 --> 00:06:58,243
'So beautiful.'

181
00:06:58,330 --> 00:07:00,376
We knew we might not have
a lot of time together

182
00:07:00,463 --> 00:07:03,466
but it wasn't supposed
to end like this.

183
00:07:03,553 --> 00:07:05,425
According to his calendar
your husband had

184
00:07:05,512 --> 00:07:06,904
multiple appointments for
treatments

185
00:07:06,991 --> 00:07:08,689
over the past few weeks.

186
00:07:08,776 --> 00:07:10,255
Who was he being treated by?

187
00:07:10,342 --> 00:07:12,606
Uh, Dr. Scheck.

188
00:07:12,693 --> 00:07:16,000
Archer was taking part in an
experimental drug protocol.

189
00:07:16,087 --> 00:07:17,828
Human guinea pig time.

190
00:07:21,528 --> 00:07:23,617
Uh, can I help you
find something?

191
00:07:23,704 --> 00:07:25,793
Oh, I'm just fishing around.

192
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:28,578
Who is this handsome young man
standing off to the side here?

193
00:07:28,665 --> 00:07:30,188
That's my brother Aden.

194
00:07:30,275 --> 00:07:32,495
Archer's oldest.

195
00:07:32,582 --> 00:07:34,976
- Where's Aden?
- Oh, no idea.

196
00:07:35,063 --> 00:07:38,501
He left the house after
a fight with Archer last year.

197
00:07:38,588 --> 00:07:41,112
They had a...
contentious relationship.

198
00:07:41,199 --> 00:07:43,201
How would you know?

199
00:07:43,288 --> 00:07:44,942
You didn't even know dad then.

200
00:07:48,859 --> 00:07:50,992
[Helen]
'She'll be okay.'

201
00:07:51,079 --> 00:07:53,821
It's been a tough day.
You can imagine.

202
00:07:54,996 --> 00:07:55,953
Excuse me.

203
00:07:56,040 --> 00:07:58,390
Mm-hmm.

204
00:07:58,478 --> 00:08:00,654
Mrs. Braddock, can you think of
anybody

205
00:08:00,741 --> 00:08:02,786
who may have wanted
to harm your husband?

206
00:08:03,613 --> 00:08:05,528
No. I can't.

207
00:08:06,747 --> 00:08:08,618
[dramatic music]

208
00:08:10,707 --> 00:08:13,362
[crying softly]
Uh..

209
00:08:13,449 --> 00:08:16,496
Do you mind if I
help myself to some tea?

210
00:08:16,583 --> 00:08:18,541
Sure.

211
00:08:18,628 --> 00:08:20,238
Thanks.

212
00:08:23,154 --> 00:08:25,679
[clattering]

213
00:08:29,770 --> 00:08:31,075
I'm trying to figure
something out.

214
00:08:31,162 --> 00:08:33,077
You might be able
to help me. Um..

215
00:08:33,164 --> 00:08:36,777
Clearly, your father liked to
live in the lap of luxury here

216
00:08:36,864 --> 00:08:39,823
but when I saw him,
he looked like he was

217
00:08:39,910 --> 00:08:43,261
headed on a pilgrimage
to an ashram in the Punjab.

218
00:08:43,348 --> 00:08:45,002
Yep.

219
00:08:45,089 --> 00:08:48,658
All hemp and natural fibers.

220
00:08:48,745 --> 00:08:51,661
'He got into it
a couple weeks ago.'

221
00:08:51,748 --> 00:08:54,403
He said that he wanted
his outside to bear testimony

222
00:08:54,490 --> 00:08:57,754
to what was happening
in the inside.

223
00:08:57,841 --> 00:09:00,931
Wh-what was happening
on the inside?

224
00:09:01,018 --> 00:09:03,804
My dad was getting healthy.

225
00:09:03,891 --> 00:09:07,329
The cancer was going away.

226
00:09:07,416 --> 00:09:08,548
That's what he said.

227
00:09:10,071 --> 00:09:11,115
Wrong.

228
00:09:11,202 --> 00:09:12,464
Interesting.

229
00:09:12,552 --> 00:09:14,554
Do you know who killed
your father?

230
00:09:14,641 --> 00:09:17,339
If I did,
they wouldn't be breathing.

231
00:09:17,426 --> 00:09:18,514
That's a dangerous path.

232
00:09:20,777 --> 00:09:21,952
Did Aden love him?

233
00:09:22,039 --> 00:09:22,997
Yeah.

234
00:09:24,955 --> 00:09:27,305
It's just..

235
00:09:27,392 --> 00:09:29,394
complicated.

236
00:09:29,481 --> 00:09:31,179
Your father kicked him out,
didn't he?

237
00:09:31,266 --> 00:09:33,268
My dad is
a recovering alcoholic.

238
00:09:35,270 --> 00:09:36,401
Was.

239
00:09:38,186 --> 00:09:41,276
It runs in the family...
addiction.

240
00:09:41,363 --> 00:09:44,148
For Aden, it was drugs.

241
00:09:44,235 --> 00:09:46,281
Dad said that Aden
had to hit bottom

242
00:09:46,368 --> 00:09:47,674
before he could rise up.

243
00:09:47,761 --> 00:09:49,632
[kettle whistling]

244
00:09:51,025 --> 00:09:51,982
Music to my ears.

245
00:09:54,202 --> 00:09:57,118
[whistling continues]

246
00:09:57,205 --> 00:09:58,598
Where would I
find the tea?

247
00:09:58,685 --> 00:10:00,077
First cabinet.

248
00:10:00,774 --> 00:10:02,427
Okay.

249
00:10:02,514 --> 00:10:04,908
[inhales deeply]
'That's dad's tea.'

250
00:10:04,995 --> 00:10:06,170
[rattles]

251
00:10:07,389 --> 00:10:08,520
[lid pops]

252
00:10:08,608 --> 00:10:09,565
[inhales deeply]
Ew!

253
00:10:09,652 --> 00:10:12,002
[laughs]

254
00:10:12,089 --> 00:10:14,265
Yeah, I bet you never
smelled that before.

255
00:10:14,352 --> 00:10:16,746
No, not since this morning.

256
00:10:16,833 --> 00:10:18,052
Where did your father
find this?

257
00:10:18,139 --> 00:10:20,054
No idea.

258
00:10:20,141 --> 00:10:22,752
It was his new fav.

259
00:10:22,839 --> 00:10:26,277
Jane...we need to go and talk
to Braddock's doctor.

260
00:10:26,364 --> 00:10:28,018
Is that the smell from, uh--

261
00:10:28,105 --> 00:10:29,890
Yes, it is.

262
00:10:29,977 --> 00:10:32,283
You, uh..

263
00:10:32,370 --> 00:10:34,416
...might want to bury that.

264
00:10:34,503 --> 00:10:35,504
Deep.

265
00:10:35,591 --> 00:10:36,810
[chuckles]
'Bye.'

266
00:10:41,249 --> 00:10:43,164
The victim's son
had a rap sheet.

267
00:10:44,252 --> 00:10:46,297
Aden Braddock.

268
00:10:46,384 --> 00:10:48,473
Disorderly conduct,
misdemeanor theft.

269
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:49,910
Looks like the kid
went a little bit wild

270
00:10:49,997 --> 00:10:51,215
after his dad
threw him out.

271
00:10:51,302 --> 00:10:52,782
- Do we know where he lives?
- No.

272
00:10:52,869 --> 00:10:54,262
But the social security
trace shows him working

273
00:10:54,349 --> 00:10:56,046
at a banquet hall
in Agua Blanca.

274
00:10:56,133 --> 00:10:57,395
We also got his cell phone
number, too.

275
00:10:59,310 --> 00:11:02,139
Wait, I've that number before
in the victim's call log.

276
00:11:02,226 --> 00:11:03,663
[mouse clicking]

277
00:11:05,403 --> 00:11:07,928
Archer Braddock called
his son at 7:45 p.m.

278
00:11:08,015 --> 00:11:09,190
It was the last call he made.

279
00:11:09,277 --> 00:11:10,670
And Braddock's wife told Lisbon

280
00:11:10,757 --> 00:11:11,671
that they had
a contentious relationship.

281
00:11:13,585 --> 00:11:15,762
Oh. Good morning, Cho.

282
00:11:15,849 --> 00:11:17,546
- Hey.
- Everything okay?

283
00:11:17,633 --> 00:11:19,069
I just, uh,
overslept my alarm.

284
00:11:19,156 --> 00:11:20,375
Yeah, well, don't get
too comfortable.

285
00:11:20,462 --> 00:11:21,942
We got a lead
on the victim's son.

286
00:11:22,029 --> 00:11:23,683
- Catch you up on the way out.
- Alright.

287
00:11:23,770 --> 00:11:24,858
[cap pops]

288
00:11:27,774 --> 00:11:29,689
[dramatic music]

289
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,916
[music continues]

290
00:11:40,003 --> 00:11:41,439
CBI. Looking
for Aden Braddock.

291
00:11:43,877 --> 00:11:44,834
'That guy.'

292
00:11:47,968 --> 00:11:50,274
[tray clatters]
'Stop, Aden.'

293
00:11:50,361 --> 00:11:51,972
[glasses shatter]

294
00:11:52,059 --> 00:11:54,931
[dramatic music]

295
00:11:56,367 --> 00:11:57,542
Stop! Police!

296
00:11:59,631 --> 00:12:00,676
[groans]

297
00:12:00,763 --> 00:12:02,678
[trays clatter]

298
00:12:02,765 --> 00:12:05,507
[intense music]

299
00:12:08,466 --> 00:12:11,382
[grunts]
Damn it.

300
00:12:17,214 --> 00:12:20,087
[indistinct conversations]

301
00:12:22,089 --> 00:12:24,047
Hi.

302
00:12:24,134 --> 00:12:26,310
Oh, you-you must be
the CBI people.

303
00:12:26,397 --> 00:12:28,269
I'm Betsy Firth,
nurse administrator.

304
00:12:28,356 --> 00:12:29,574
I'm Patrick Jane.

305
00:12:29,661 --> 00:12:31,794
I'm a consulting, uh,
consultant.

306
00:12:31,881 --> 00:12:33,143
And I'm Agent Lisbon.

307
00:12:33,230 --> 00:12:34,710
She's a detective.

308
00:12:34,797 --> 00:12:37,321
Dr. Scheck will be
with you in a minute.

309
00:12:37,408 --> 00:12:40,150
We were all very saddened
to hear about Archer Braddock.

310
00:12:40,237 --> 00:12:41,499
I pulled his files in case

311
00:12:41,586 --> 00:12:43,327
Dr. Scheck
needs to consult them.

312
00:12:43,414 --> 00:12:46,330
Well, thank you, Betsy.
That's very efficient of you.

313
00:12:46,417 --> 00:12:48,071
Mm. Well, we try.
Come on in.

314
00:12:49,812 --> 00:12:53,468
Dr. Scheck, the CBI people
are here to see you.

315
00:12:53,555 --> 00:12:55,165
[Lisbon]
'We just wanted to ask you
a few questions'

316
00:12:55,252 --> 00:12:56,645
'about Archer Braddock .'

317
00:12:56,732 --> 00:12:59,300
Oh, I was very sorry
to hear about that.

318
00:12:59,387 --> 00:13:01,650
We deal with death every day
here but not like that.

319
00:13:01,737 --> 00:13:03,260
It's...awful.

320
00:13:03,347 --> 00:13:06,176
Awful, yes...uh, what's the drug
you're testing here?

321
00:13:06,263 --> 00:13:08,483
Osevent. It's a
bone cancer treatment.

322
00:13:08,570 --> 00:13:11,007
It has the potential
to extend millions of lives.

323
00:13:11,094 --> 00:13:13,444
And earn millions of dollars.

324
00:13:13,531 --> 00:13:15,316
Well, that's not
why we developed it.

325
00:13:15,403 --> 00:13:17,622
No? Why not?

326
00:13:17,709 --> 00:13:19,320
Do you save lives
for the money?

327
00:13:19,407 --> 00:13:22,671
Well, folks are pretty much dead
by the time we show up.

328
00:13:22,758 --> 00:13:24,934
Uh, best we can hope for
is to catch the killer.

329
00:13:25,021 --> 00:13:26,414
Did you kill Archer Braddock?

330
00:13:29,025 --> 00:13:30,418
I'll be talking toyou
from now on.

331
00:13:30,505 --> 00:13:32,333
Yes. I just have
a few questions

332
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:33,943
about Mr. Braddock's
last appointment.

333
00:13:34,030 --> 00:13:35,945
Hmm. Okay. Uh, Betsy.

334
00:13:36,032 --> 00:13:38,687
I laid out the Braddock file
in consult room 2.

335
00:13:38,774 --> 00:13:39,819
Follow me.

336
00:13:42,560 --> 00:13:45,346
I'll just, uh,
wait out here.

337
00:13:45,433 --> 00:13:47,957
[speaking inaudibly]

338
00:13:51,439 --> 00:13:54,659
The Braddock file
...daily report.

339
00:13:54,746 --> 00:13:58,011
And when you're done, you need
to sign these intake forms.

340
00:13:58,098 --> 00:13:59,795
[pen clicks]
Any new patients today?

341
00:13:59,882 --> 00:14:02,102
Four. Newly cleared
and qualified.

342
00:14:02,189 --> 00:14:03,364
I don't know how you do it.

343
00:14:05,409 --> 00:14:06,846
So how can I help you,
agent?

344
00:14:06,933 --> 00:14:08,238
Uh, Mr. Braddock's
last appointment

345
00:14:08,325 --> 00:14:09,718
was he acting strangely at all?

346
00:14:09,805 --> 00:14:11,241
Not that I recall, no.

347
00:14:11,328 --> 00:14:13,113
Did he say anything
unusual

348
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:15,376
or mention where he was going
after the appointment?

349
00:14:15,463 --> 00:14:16,638
Let me check his file.

350
00:14:16,725 --> 00:14:18,248
It was just
yesterday afternoon.

351
00:14:18,335 --> 00:14:20,424
Yesterday? No, no, no. Uh.

352
00:14:20,511 --> 00:14:22,426
Braddock's last drug treatment
was two days ago in the morning.

353
00:14:22,513 --> 00:14:24,254
According to his calendar,
he had a treatment

354
00:14:24,341 --> 00:14:26,517
yesterday at 4:17 p.m.

355
00:14:26,604 --> 00:14:27,867
Well, not a medical treatment.

356
00:14:27,954 --> 00:14:29,825
Braddock was under
my exclusive care.

357
00:14:29,912 --> 00:14:31,696
And not to be indelicate, agent

358
00:14:31,783 --> 00:14:33,437
but when do I get access
to the body?

359
00:14:33,524 --> 00:14:35,178
- Access?
- I need to examine it.

360
00:14:35,265 --> 00:14:37,093
Obtain tissue samples
for my research.

361
00:14:37,180 --> 00:14:38,442
I-it's all part of the agreement

362
00:14:38,529 --> 00:14:40,227
our patients sign
when they join the study.

363
00:14:40,314 --> 00:14:42,055
Uh, you're gonna have to
take that up with the coroner

364
00:14:42,142 --> 00:14:43,491
and she's working on
another case.

365
00:14:43,578 --> 00:14:45,188
Might keep her busy
for a few days.

366
00:14:45,275 --> 00:14:47,190
I see.

367
00:14:47,277 --> 00:14:49,540
- Excuse me.
- Oh.

368
00:14:55,285 --> 00:14:56,852
Allow me.

369
00:14:56,939 --> 00:15:00,682
- Oh, well, thank you, handsome.
- Hmm.

370
00:15:00,769 --> 00:15:03,380
Take my advice.
Don't get cancer.

371
00:15:03,467 --> 00:15:05,165
Well, that sounds like
good advice.

372
00:15:05,252 --> 00:15:07,950
Although you do seem spry
despite all.

373
00:15:08,037 --> 00:15:09,430
You know, why not?

374
00:15:09,517 --> 00:15:11,388
Western wisdom says
I should have been dead

375
00:15:11,475 --> 00:15:13,260
nine months ago,
but look.

376
00:15:13,347 --> 00:15:14,870
I made it
to the bonus round.

377
00:15:14,957 --> 00:15:16,350
You credit the drugs here?

378
00:15:16,437 --> 00:15:19,353
- Could be the drugs.
- Could be the tea.

379
00:15:19,440 --> 00:15:20,745
You know about the tea?

380
00:15:20,832 --> 00:15:22,791
Yeah, I could smell it
in your handbag.

381
00:15:22,878 --> 00:15:25,925
No, no, no, no need, I got a
very strong whiff of it

382
00:15:26,012 --> 00:15:27,578
in Archer Braddock's kitchen.

383
00:15:27,665 --> 00:15:29,015
I heard about Archer
this morning.

384
00:15:29,102 --> 00:15:30,799
Were-were you a friend?

385
00:15:30,886 --> 00:15:33,497
Oh, no, no, no. I'm helping
the police solve the murder.

386
00:15:33,584 --> 00:15:36,544
Oh. Well, I was the one
who told Archer about the tea.

387
00:15:36,631 --> 00:15:39,764
I saw him a few weeks back,
and he wasn't looking so good.

388
00:15:39,851 --> 00:15:42,637
You know, he had this
air of finality hanging over him

389
00:15:42,724 --> 00:15:44,465
like a piano on a wire.

390
00:15:44,552 --> 00:15:47,120
Mm-hm...where would
one buy a tin

391
00:15:47,207 --> 00:15:49,644
of that stinky tea
if they had to?

392
00:15:49,731 --> 00:15:51,951
[Lisbon]
'Who closes shop
in the middle of the day?'

393
00:15:52,038 --> 00:15:53,387
[Patrick]
'Mmm.'

394
00:15:54,518 --> 00:15:56,738
[instrumental music]

395
00:15:57,913 --> 00:15:59,871
[door clicks open]

396
00:15:59,959 --> 00:16:01,221
They probably just forgot
to flip the sign.

397
00:16:06,182 --> 00:16:07,575
Hello?

398
00:16:07,662 --> 00:16:09,533
Is anyone here?

399
00:16:12,449 --> 00:16:14,103
What's the goal here,
anyway?

400
00:16:14,190 --> 00:16:16,323
So Braddock drinks
stinky tea.

401
00:16:16,410 --> 00:16:18,238
People who are dying
will try anything.

402
00:16:18,325 --> 00:16:20,805
Well, the stuff that Braddock
tried was working

403
00:16:20,892 --> 00:16:22,503
'or so he thought.'

404
00:16:22,590 --> 00:16:24,505
What, you think that has
something to do with his murder?

405
00:16:24,592 --> 00:16:27,856
No idea, but if Braddock
found the cure for cancer

406
00:16:27,943 --> 00:16:29,249
I want in on it.

407
00:16:29,336 --> 00:16:30,772
- Good luck with that.
- Shh!

408
00:16:30,859 --> 00:16:33,775
[muffled groaning]

409
00:16:35,516 --> 00:16:37,735
[whimpering and groaning]

410
00:16:37,822 --> 00:16:39,476
[Patrick mutters]

411
00:16:40,869 --> 00:16:43,480
[woman groaning loudly]

412
00:16:43,567 --> 00:16:45,352
'Help me.'

413
00:16:45,439 --> 00:16:47,223
[woman crying]

414
00:16:47,310 --> 00:16:50,487
- 'Help me. Helpme.'
- Stay back.

415
00:16:51,706 --> 00:16:52,663
[panting]
Help me!

416
00:16:52,750 --> 00:16:54,752
[Lisbon]
'Police!'

417
00:16:54,839 --> 00:16:59,192
Please. Calm your spirits.
It's okay. Everything's fine.

418
00:16:59,279 --> 00:17:01,107
[woman panting]
Shh.

419
00:17:01,194 --> 00:17:03,239
- What are you doing?
- What are youdoing?

420
00:17:08,679 --> 00:17:10,725
[instrumental music]

421
00:17:10,812 --> 00:17:12,640
[speaking inaudibly]

422
00:17:12,727 --> 00:17:13,945
[indistinct conversations]

423
00:17:14,033 --> 00:17:15,991
Welcome.
I'm Brother Josef.

424
00:17:16,078 --> 00:17:18,341
Oh, and I am not a hugger.

425
00:17:18,428 --> 00:17:21,214
[chuckles]
Patrick Jane. Nice to meet you.
That was a wonderful show.

426
00:17:21,301 --> 00:17:23,259
How much do you charge
for that show?

427
00:17:23,346 --> 00:17:26,045
- No show. A healing.
- Hmm.

428
00:17:26,132 --> 00:17:27,742
And my temple
accepts donations.

429
00:17:27,829 --> 00:17:29,613
Uh, it's entirely
up to you.

430
00:17:29,700 --> 00:17:33,400
Ahh. Another nonprofit.
Smart act.

431
00:17:33,487 --> 00:17:36,620
[chuckles]
I get that reaction a lot.

432
00:17:36,707 --> 00:17:38,100
But the healing powers
of the sun

433
00:17:38,187 --> 00:17:39,623
have been well-documented.

434
00:17:39,710 --> 00:17:41,625
- Then where does the tea fit?
- Tea?

435
00:17:41,712 --> 00:17:44,280
Yeah, the stinky red stuff,
curls the nose hairs.

436
00:17:44,367 --> 00:17:46,848
[chuckles]
You're talking about
the tea of clarity.

437
00:17:46,935 --> 00:17:48,719
It's pretty foul stuff.

438
00:17:48,806 --> 00:17:50,591
I had to take it
off our shelves recently.

439
00:17:50,678 --> 00:17:54,290
- Too many customer complaints.
- What's in it?

440
00:17:54,377 --> 00:17:58,947
A proprietary blend of nettles,
herbs, and possibility.

441
00:17:59,034 --> 00:18:01,080
And how much does that
go for these days?

442
00:18:01,167 --> 00:18:04,039
[chuckles]
You have me all wrong.

443
00:18:04,126 --> 00:18:05,954
I am a rich man
but you won't find

444
00:18:06,041 --> 00:18:08,522
my wealth in any bank account.

445
00:18:08,609 --> 00:18:11,307
I sell just enough teas
and holistic remedies

446
00:18:11,394 --> 00:18:13,092
to keep my temple doors open.

447
00:18:13,179 --> 00:18:15,703
And did you open them
to this man?

448
00:18:15,790 --> 00:18:17,313
Sure. Archer.

449
00:18:17,400 --> 00:18:21,187
We just performed
his final healing ceremony.

450
00:18:21,274 --> 00:18:22,753
At 4:17?

451
00:18:22,840 --> 00:18:25,495
That's when the solar flares
were at their peak.

452
00:18:25,582 --> 00:18:27,018
And what were you healing?

453
00:18:27,106 --> 00:18:28,846
His cancer, of course.

454
00:18:28,933 --> 00:18:34,069
We channel the solar corona
to target cancerous tumors.

455
00:18:34,156 --> 00:18:36,027
We had many tries
with Archer

456
00:18:36,115 --> 00:18:38,029
but we, we finally got 'em all.

457
00:18:38,117 --> 00:18:39,596
And all of his money.

458
00:18:39,683 --> 00:18:43,122
Archer didn't pay me
a penny, Mr. Jane.

459
00:18:43,209 --> 00:18:45,080
Well, except for the tea.

460
00:18:45,167 --> 00:18:46,690
Mmm. Is that why
you killed him?

461
00:18:46,777 --> 00:18:48,910
Excuse me?
What?

462
00:18:48,997 --> 00:18:51,042
Archer Braddock is dead.

463
00:18:51,130 --> 00:18:52,783
He was murdered
right after he left here.

464
00:18:52,870 --> 00:18:57,048
Oh, no. Oh, no, no, no.

465
00:18:57,136 --> 00:19:00,835
Not after all he fought through.

466
00:19:00,922 --> 00:19:03,011
After all our work.

467
00:19:03,098 --> 00:19:04,186
Oh, my God.

468
00:19:06,232 --> 00:19:07,189
[sighs]

469
00:19:09,365 --> 00:19:11,280
His-his reflexes
were better.

470
00:19:11,367 --> 00:19:13,978
His-his-his
pressure was way down.

471
00:19:14,065 --> 00:19:17,808
He had a-he had a ruddy,
healthy glow in his cheeks.

472
00:19:17,895 --> 00:19:19,767
- That part of the act?
- Hard to say.

473
00:19:19,854 --> 00:19:21,856
It's all so theatrical.

474
00:19:21,943 --> 00:19:24,554
Did Braddock say where he was
going when he was leaving?

475
00:19:24,641 --> 00:19:26,208
No, no. I-I have no idea.

476
00:19:28,471 --> 00:19:31,300
His transition from this world
was corrupted by violence.

477
00:19:31,387 --> 00:19:34,869
- I need to see his body.
- Why?

478
00:19:34,956 --> 00:19:38,351
To release his energy so he can
cross over peacefully.

479
00:19:38,438 --> 00:19:39,961
Maybe at the funeral.

480
00:19:40,048 --> 00:19:41,528
In the meantime,
you're gonna tell us

481
00:19:41,615 --> 00:19:44,270
everything that happened
yesterday.

482
00:19:44,357 --> 00:19:46,054
Of course.

483
00:19:46,141 --> 00:19:48,274
Yeah, I have that.
Okay. Sure.

484
00:19:50,145 --> 00:19:51,407
This is Cho.

485
00:19:51,494 --> 00:19:53,192
It's Summer.

486
00:19:56,717 --> 00:19:58,240
Hey.

487
00:19:58,327 --> 00:20:00,895
I just wanted to apologize
for making you late.

488
00:20:00,982 --> 00:20:03,202
It's okay. You were only
trying to help.

489
00:20:03,289 --> 00:20:05,160
How can I make it
up to you?

490
00:20:05,247 --> 00:20:07,554
[chuckles]
I'm sure you'll
think of something.

491
00:20:07,641 --> 00:20:09,686
Hey, Cho.

492
00:20:09,773 --> 00:20:11,079
I gotta go.
I'll call you later.

493
00:20:11,166 --> 00:20:12,123
Okay.

494
00:20:14,343 --> 00:20:16,389
That was Agua Blanca PD.
They found the victim's car.

495
00:20:16,476 --> 00:20:18,042
It was about a block away
from that banquet hall

496
00:20:18,129 --> 00:20:19,435
where Aden gave us the slip.

497
00:20:19,522 --> 00:20:21,132
Well, Braddock's last phone call
was to Aden.

498
00:20:21,220 --> 00:20:23,831
So maybe father and son had a
reunion that didn't go so well.

499
00:20:23,918 --> 00:20:26,137
Wouldn't be surprised.
I talked to the estate lawyer.

500
00:20:26,225 --> 00:20:27,791
Most of Braddock's money
was split into trusts

501
00:20:27,878 --> 00:20:30,359
for Liesl and Aden, payable
on their 23rd birthdays.

502
00:20:30,446 --> 00:20:32,405
Sounds normal.
Rich normal, anyway.

503
00:20:32,492 --> 00:20:34,450
Yeah, but in light
of his son's drug problems

504
00:20:34,537 --> 00:20:35,712
Braddock was in
the process of changing

505
00:20:35,799 --> 00:20:38,237
the age of Aden's trust
from 23 to 30.

506
00:20:38,324 --> 00:20:40,239
So Aden confronts Braddock.

507
00:20:40,326 --> 00:20:41,805
Argument breaks out.

508
00:20:41,892 --> 00:20:43,720
Aden chokes dad
and buries the body.

509
00:20:43,807 --> 00:20:47,115
- We need to find that kid.
- Yeah.

510
00:20:47,202 --> 00:20:50,466
Rigsby, can you hold?

511
00:20:50,553 --> 00:20:51,946
Well, he has to collect
his pay, right?

512
00:20:52,033 --> 00:20:53,426
Why don't we stake out
the banquet hall?

513
00:20:53,513 --> 00:20:55,123
Aden was a part-timer,
minimum wage.

514
00:20:55,210 --> 00:20:57,125
He's not gonna risk getting
caught for that paycheck.

515
00:20:57,212 --> 00:20:58,561
Can we track
his cell phone?

516
00:20:58,648 --> 00:21:00,041
Tried. It's powered off.

517
00:21:00,128 --> 00:21:01,608
What about the lottery scam?

518
00:21:01,695 --> 00:21:05,133
I don't know. I think
he's too smart for that.

519
00:21:05,220 --> 00:21:07,875
Hey, who's this?
Sorry to keep you waiting.

520
00:21:07,962 --> 00:21:09,093
Aden Braddock?

521
00:21:09,180 --> 00:21:11,922
Uh, yeah.
Sure, you can.

522
00:21:12,009 --> 00:21:13,837
Yeah, you can come in
and talk to us.

523
00:21:13,924 --> 00:21:14,882
Let me give you the address.

524
00:21:17,580 --> 00:21:18,581
Why'd you run, Aden?

525
00:21:18,668 --> 00:21:21,018
I don't know.

526
00:21:21,105 --> 00:21:25,066
[exhales]
Okay, I do. I was carrying
drugs, okay?

527
00:21:25,153 --> 00:21:28,112
I couldn't get popped again. It
would have been my third strike.

528
00:21:28,199 --> 00:21:30,811
So instead you'll be
charged with obstruction.

529
00:21:30,898 --> 00:21:33,292
- A felony offense.
- I wasn't thinking straight.

530
00:21:33,379 --> 00:21:36,120
I'd just found out
my father was killed.

531
00:21:36,207 --> 00:21:38,253
Liesl called me.

532
00:21:38,340 --> 00:21:41,212
Look.

533
00:21:41,300 --> 00:21:42,779
I need you
to come clean with me.

534
00:21:42,866 --> 00:21:45,304
When was the last time
you talked to your father?

535
00:21:45,391 --> 00:21:47,131
I don't know. Months?

536
00:21:47,218 --> 00:21:49,743
Okay, look.

537
00:21:49,830 --> 00:21:52,267
We're getting off
to a bad start here.

538
00:21:52,354 --> 00:21:54,574
He called you
at 7:45 last night.

539
00:21:57,054 --> 00:21:59,622
[Archer Braddock]
Hey, kiddo, it's dad.
It's been a while.

540
00:21:59,709 --> 00:22:02,277
I didn't wanna hear it.

541
00:22:02,364 --> 00:22:03,757
You said "Talk to."

542
00:22:03,844 --> 00:22:05,802
That's a message
and I didn't wanna hear it.

543
00:22:05,889 --> 00:22:07,543
You know whose car was found
a block away from your job?

544
00:22:07,630 --> 00:22:09,458
I wasn't working last night.
Ask anybody.

545
00:22:09,545 --> 00:22:10,851
Well, maybe he thought
youwereworking

546
00:22:10,938 --> 00:22:12,069
and someone followed him.

547
00:22:12,156 --> 00:22:14,681
Who might that be?

548
00:22:14,768 --> 00:22:16,726
Oh, no.

549
00:22:21,209 --> 00:22:22,863
- I have this friend.
- Friend?

550
00:22:22,950 --> 00:22:25,256
Dealer.
Mike Tran.

551
00:22:25,344 --> 00:22:26,388
Lately, he's been
asking a lot of questions

552
00:22:26,475 --> 00:22:28,608
about my dad, like..

553
00:22:28,695 --> 00:22:30,349
...how much he's worth
and what kind of cars he sells.

554
00:22:30,436 --> 00:22:33,221
- And you told him?
- Why wouldn't I?

555
00:22:33,308 --> 00:22:36,093
I hated my father.

556
00:22:36,180 --> 00:22:38,444
He cut me off and..

557
00:22:38,531 --> 00:22:39,445
...yeah.

558
00:22:39,532 --> 00:22:41,751
I'm not talking about money.

559
00:22:43,884 --> 00:22:45,364
He left me.

560
00:22:50,543 --> 00:22:52,936
[instrumental music]

561
00:22:53,023 --> 00:22:55,896
No sign of Tran.
Cho?

562
00:22:55,983 --> 00:22:58,246
'How's it looking back there?'

563
00:23:01,684 --> 00:23:04,208
I got nothing
going on back here.

564
00:23:04,295 --> 00:23:07,168
Aden's dealer's
a nasty piece of business.

565
00:23:07,255 --> 00:23:09,562
Two charges aggravated assault

566
00:23:09,649 --> 00:23:11,564
intent to distribute meth

567
00:23:11,651 --> 00:23:13,914
and he choked out
a probation officer.

568
00:23:14,001 --> 00:23:15,742
It's worth checking out.

569
00:23:17,874 --> 00:23:19,833
You sure he'll be here?

570
00:23:19,920 --> 00:23:23,793
His baby-mother says he was
dropping off some money today.

571
00:23:23,880 --> 00:23:25,273
He can be your role model.

572
00:23:25,360 --> 00:23:26,317
[scoffs]

573
00:23:33,803 --> 00:23:36,676
Did you know your husband
was seeing Brother Josef?

574
00:23:36,763 --> 00:23:39,156
Archer's cancer made him..

575
00:23:39,243 --> 00:23:41,158
...a target for
quite a few con men

576
00:23:41,245 --> 00:23:42,943
'and alternative remedies.'

577
00:23:43,030 --> 00:23:45,119
Why didn't you tell us?

578
00:23:45,206 --> 00:23:48,905
Forgive me for trying
to maintain a man's dignity.

579
00:23:48,992 --> 00:23:50,603
We have a cancer
charity in our name

580
00:23:50,690 --> 00:23:52,431
that we've raised
a lot of money for.

581
00:23:52,518 --> 00:23:54,998
Did you know that
he was getting better?

582
00:23:55,085 --> 00:23:56,347
Yeah.

583
00:23:56,435 --> 00:23:57,653
The same way
he was getting better

584
00:23:57,740 --> 00:23:59,916
after the oxygen therapy
in Germany

585
00:24:00,003 --> 00:24:03,877
or the psychic surgery
in Brazil.

586
00:24:03,964 --> 00:24:06,836
It was a constant
roller coaster ride with Archer.

587
00:24:14,235 --> 00:24:17,847
Chemo, huh?
Kills the appetite.

588
00:24:17,934 --> 00:24:20,807
- How about a spot of tea?
- Oh, that sounds lovely.

589
00:24:21,590 --> 00:24:23,374
You got it.

590
00:24:26,769 --> 00:24:29,642
[instrumental music]

591
00:24:34,560 --> 00:24:37,519
Suspect has arrived.
Time to move.

592
00:24:49,444 --> 00:24:51,272
Cho?
You copy?

593
00:24:51,359 --> 00:24:54,275
[glass shattering]

594
00:24:54,362 --> 00:24:57,496
Sounds like a fight.
I'm going in.

595
00:24:58,584 --> 00:24:59,976
Go! Go!

596
00:25:02,413 --> 00:25:03,371
Go!

597
00:25:03,458 --> 00:25:05,112
Police!

598
00:25:05,199 --> 00:25:06,722
[crying]

599
00:25:06,809 --> 00:25:08,942
You're safe.
It's okay.

600
00:25:09,029 --> 00:25:11,074
Is he there?

601
00:25:11,161 --> 00:25:13,120
[gunshot]

602
00:25:13,207 --> 00:25:15,252
[grunting]

603
00:25:15,339 --> 00:25:17,428
[gunshot]

604
00:25:17,516 --> 00:25:18,429
[Kimball Cho]
'Stay down!'

605
00:25:23,957 --> 00:25:25,741
Weapon's secure.

606
00:25:25,828 --> 00:25:27,264
- You good?
- Yeah.

607
00:25:29,049 --> 00:25:30,267
[Kimball Cho]
'Get up.'

608
00:25:33,575 --> 00:25:35,316
Guess it was my lucky day.

609
00:25:44,934 --> 00:25:47,371
Mrs. Braddock is still
upstairs waiting for her tea.

610
00:25:47,458 --> 00:25:49,069
What are you doing?

611
00:25:49,156 --> 00:25:52,551
Oh, just, uh, enjoying
the calm before the storm.

612
00:25:52,638 --> 00:25:54,117
You only made
one cup of tea.

613
00:25:54,204 --> 00:25:57,556
Mm. I needed to keep
those tea bags dry.

614
00:25:57,643 --> 00:26:00,820
I'm not gonna like this, am I?

615
00:26:00,907 --> 00:26:03,605
You're gonna love it.

616
00:26:10,351 --> 00:26:11,439
[Lisbon]
'Oh, ho!'

617
00:26:13,484 --> 00:26:16,400
[instrumental music]

618
00:26:42,252 --> 00:26:44,951
You may wanna cover your ears.

619
00:26:47,301 --> 00:26:50,217
[beeping]

620
00:26:56,266 --> 00:26:58,442
Liesl!

621
00:26:58,529 --> 00:26:59,705
Liesl!

622
00:26:59,792 --> 00:27:01,707
Somebody help me!

623
00:27:03,970 --> 00:27:05,232
Showtime.

624
00:27:06,407 --> 00:27:09,323
[beeping]

625
00:27:11,151 --> 00:27:12,892
Fire!

626
00:27:15,285 --> 00:27:17,940
She moves pretty good
for a cancer patient.

627
00:27:18,027 --> 00:27:19,550
Oh, it's a miracle.

628
00:27:19,638 --> 00:27:22,118
Sister Helen has been healed!

629
00:27:22,205 --> 00:27:23,903
Ma'am!

630
00:27:23,990 --> 00:27:25,861
Ma'am!

631
00:27:30,213 --> 00:27:32,128
What kind of woman fakes cancer?

632
00:27:32,215 --> 00:27:34,914
Lots of women go to
extremes to meet men.

633
00:27:35,001 --> 00:27:38,787
Extremes, yes.
Cancer support groups, no.

634
00:27:38,874 --> 00:27:41,877
I was lucky to have Archer
for the time that I did.

635
00:27:41,964 --> 00:27:45,576
He opened doors in me that
I didn't even know existed.

636
00:27:45,664 --> 00:27:48,449
He was caring,
generous, spiritual.

637
00:27:48,536 --> 00:27:51,670
Rich...and dying fast.

638
00:27:52,409 --> 00:27:54,020
The perfect man.

639
00:27:54,107 --> 00:27:57,327
How did you know?
That I didn't have cancer?

640
00:27:57,414 --> 00:27:59,721
Well, you lacked a..

641
00:28:01,505 --> 00:28:03,899
'...an air of finality.'

642
00:28:03,986 --> 00:28:05,422
Plus you shaved your head

643
00:28:05,509 --> 00:28:07,337
but your vanity prevented you

644
00:28:07,424 --> 00:28:09,818
from losing the eyebrows.

645
00:28:09,905 --> 00:28:11,820
Devil's in the details.

646
00:28:14,170 --> 00:28:16,956
Braddock's insurance policy.

647
00:28:17,043 --> 00:28:20,524
It was recently changed
to add a new beneficiary.

648
00:28:20,611 --> 00:28:23,179
- You.
- I didn't kill Archer.

649
00:28:23,266 --> 00:28:26,139
You had 5 million reasons
to want him dead.

650
00:28:26,226 --> 00:28:29,011
Archer had a month left, maybe.

651
00:28:29,098 --> 00:28:32,014
Why would I risk losing all
of that money to take his life?

652
00:28:32,101 --> 00:28:34,538
I don't know.
Doyou?

653
00:28:34,625 --> 00:28:36,410
She's telling the truth.
You can go.

654
00:28:36,497 --> 00:28:38,194
- No. She can't.
- You can't.

655
00:28:38,281 --> 00:28:40,109
- Why not?
- I'm charging you with fraud.

656
00:28:40,196 --> 00:28:41,676
What?

657
00:28:41,763 --> 00:28:44,113
You're not gonna see a dime
of that insurance money.

658
00:28:44,200 --> 00:28:46,420
And your face is gonna be
plastered all over the news.

659
00:28:46,507 --> 00:28:48,074
But at least
you'll have your eyebrows.

660
00:28:48,161 --> 00:28:50,293
Let's see how many
dates you get then.

661
00:28:50,380 --> 00:28:52,339
Yeah.

662
00:28:52,426 --> 00:28:53,949
- Do you feel better?
- I do.

663
00:28:54,036 --> 00:28:55,908
Yeah, good.
Now it's my turn.

664
00:28:55,995 --> 00:28:57,431
I have a plan
to catch our killer.

665
00:28:57,518 --> 00:28:59,520
Can't wait to hear that.

666
00:28:59,607 --> 00:29:02,523
[instrumental music]

667
00:29:16,276 --> 00:29:18,234
He looks so thin..

668
00:29:21,760 --> 00:29:23,457
...fragile.

669
00:29:23,544 --> 00:29:24,893
When was the last time
you saw him?

670
00:29:24,980 --> 00:29:26,373
A year ago.

671
00:29:26,460 --> 00:29:28,723
When he told us
about the diagnosis.

672
00:29:30,681 --> 00:29:33,772
It ended with us fighting
and Liesl crying.

673
00:29:37,253 --> 00:29:39,560
How does that happen?

674
00:29:39,647 --> 00:29:41,170
Ran down Tran's alibi.

675
00:29:41,257 --> 00:29:43,433
But he was 40 miles away
when your father was killed.

676
00:29:43,520 --> 00:29:45,740
- So Tran didn't do it?
- No.

677
00:29:45,827 --> 00:29:47,307
But we do have a suspect.

678
00:29:47,394 --> 00:29:49,570
One whose alibi doesn't hold up.

679
00:29:49,657 --> 00:29:51,659
Does the name
"Dolores" ring any bells?

680
00:29:51,746 --> 00:29:53,269
The waitress
at the banquet hall?

681
00:29:53,356 --> 00:29:55,010
[Rigsby]
'You told me you weren't
at work last night'

682
00:29:55,097 --> 00:29:56,490
but Dolores says
you stopped by around eight

683
00:29:56,577 --> 00:29:58,274
to borrow some money.

684
00:29:58,361 --> 00:29:59,667
- You lied.
- No, I didn't.

685
00:29:59,754 --> 00:30:01,495
I told you I wasn't working.

686
00:30:01,582 --> 00:30:03,149
I didn't say
I wasn't at work.

687
00:30:03,236 --> 00:30:06,195
You know, it's all lies
and half-truths with you.

688
00:30:06,282 --> 00:30:07,718
How are we supposed to
know what to believe?

689
00:30:07,806 --> 00:30:11,070
Look, I needed money
for the drugs, okay?

690
00:30:11,157 --> 00:30:13,115
And I needed drugs
after I saw that my dad called.

691
00:30:13,202 --> 00:30:16,162
He had this way of
making me feel so small.

692
00:30:16,249 --> 00:30:17,685
Not anymore.

693
00:30:19,469 --> 00:30:21,471
I didn't kill him.

694
00:30:23,169 --> 00:30:25,519
- You have to believe me.
- No, we don't.

695
00:30:25,606 --> 00:30:26,825
Give me your hands.

696
00:30:34,310 --> 00:30:36,312
Science never sleeps
around here, does it?

697
00:30:38,140 --> 00:30:40,577
Mr. Jane,
how may I help you?

698
00:30:40,664 --> 00:30:43,972
Well, I don't need
your services...thankfully.

699
00:30:44,059 --> 00:30:45,234
Even if I did,
I'm not sure

700
00:30:45,321 --> 00:30:47,280
this is the first place,
I'd come.

701
00:30:47,367 --> 00:30:49,151
I met this fellow,
Brother Josef

702
00:30:49,238 --> 00:30:52,676
who can, uh, totally
eradicate bone cancer.

703
00:30:52,763 --> 00:30:54,200
Really? How so?

704
00:30:54,287 --> 00:30:56,115
The power of positive thinking.

705
00:30:56,202 --> 00:30:57,290
Naturally.

706
00:30:57,377 --> 00:30:59,248
That's right, naturally.

707
00:30:59,335 --> 00:31:01,511
No drugs whatsoever.

708
00:31:01,598 --> 00:31:04,471
That's how
he cured Archer Braddock.

709
00:31:04,558 --> 00:31:07,866
- Is that what this man claims?
- Yeah.

710
00:31:07,953 --> 00:31:08,954
Betsy, pull up
Braddock's file.

711
00:31:09,041 --> 00:31:10,346
Uh, certainly.

712
00:31:10,433 --> 00:31:11,957
'Let me show you
something, Mr. Jane.'

713
00:31:14,089 --> 00:31:16,439
This is Braddock's last MRI,
taken two weeks ago.

714
00:31:16,526 --> 00:31:18,006
You see all those white spots?

715
00:31:18,093 --> 00:31:20,879
- Yeah, looks like a snowstorm.
- It's cancer.

716
00:31:20,966 --> 00:31:23,490
It spread from his bones and was
working its way to his lungs.

717
00:31:23,577 --> 00:31:25,231
He had five weeks, max.

718
00:31:25,318 --> 00:31:26,493
Oh, this was taken
two weeks ago.

719
00:31:26,580 --> 00:31:28,669
Maybe that cancer's cleared up.

720
00:31:28,756 --> 00:31:30,758
The mind is
a very powerful tool.

721
00:31:30,845 --> 00:31:31,977
Well, I'm happy to
show you personally

722
00:31:32,064 --> 00:31:33,717
when I'm granted
access to the body.

723
00:31:33,804 --> 00:31:35,110
Where are we at
with my request?

724
00:31:35,197 --> 00:31:37,199
Oh, I still can't
reach the coroner.

725
00:31:37,286 --> 00:31:40,463
Everyone wants to look
inside that body.

726
00:31:40,550 --> 00:31:42,465
Brother Josef
had the same request.

727
00:31:42,552 --> 00:31:43,858
Did you approve it?

728
00:31:43,945 --> 00:31:45,077
Well, I'll tell you what
I told Brother Josef.

729
00:31:45,164 --> 00:31:47,035
It's out of my hands.

730
00:31:47,122 --> 00:31:49,255
But my boss has called in
a forensic pathologist

731
00:31:49,342 --> 00:31:50,560
from Sacramento.

732
00:31:50,647 --> 00:31:52,345
He'll be performing
a full autopsy

733
00:31:52,432 --> 00:31:53,607
first thing in
the morning.

734
00:31:53,694 --> 00:31:55,000
I'll let you know
what he finds.

735
00:31:55,087 --> 00:31:56,392
Well..

736
00:32:08,361 --> 00:32:10,189
Oh, hey.

737
00:32:10,276 --> 00:32:11,755
I wanted to, uh, show you this.

738
00:32:11,842 --> 00:32:13,409
We need to talk about
what happened today.

739
00:32:13,496 --> 00:32:16,238
I was late covering
your back. I'm sorry.

740
00:32:16,325 --> 00:32:18,762
- You're sorry?
- It won't happen again.

741
00:32:18,849 --> 00:32:21,330
Cho, you saved my life.

742
00:32:23,942 --> 00:32:26,292
You were there
when I needed you.

743
00:32:26,379 --> 00:32:28,381
I owe you...big time.

744
00:32:29,773 --> 00:32:31,732
No.

745
00:32:31,819 --> 00:32:33,429
You don't owe me anything.

746
00:32:35,170 --> 00:32:36,563
Okay.

747
00:32:44,310 --> 00:32:45,615
This is too creepy.

748
00:32:45,702 --> 00:32:47,661
Killers like creepy.

749
00:32:47,748 --> 00:32:49,663
I can't feel my feet.
They're freezing.

750
00:32:49,750 --> 00:32:51,012
Be patient.

751
00:32:52,840 --> 00:32:54,581
Has it ever occurred to you that
maybe your theory was wrong?

752
00:32:54,668 --> 00:32:56,409
Nope.

753
00:32:56,496 --> 00:32:57,932
Braddock's killer buried him
because there was something

754
00:32:58,019 --> 00:32:59,368
about the body,
he didn't want people to know.

755
00:32:59,455 --> 00:33:01,544
So if we put out
the word of an autopsy

756
00:33:01,631 --> 00:33:04,286
then we force the killer
to try again.

757
00:33:04,373 --> 00:33:06,462
If we don't die
of frostbite first.

758
00:33:06,549 --> 00:33:08,160
Can you wiggle your toes?

759
00:33:08,247 --> 00:33:10,075
Because if you can wiggle
your toes, you're fine.

760
00:33:10,162 --> 00:33:11,206
- I can't move them--
- Shh. Shh.

761
00:33:11,293 --> 00:33:13,208
Someone is comin'.

762
00:33:17,821 --> 00:33:19,127
Oh!

763
00:33:19,214 --> 00:33:21,956
Surprise, surprise.

764
00:33:29,181 --> 00:33:31,922
You're under arrest for
the murder of Archer Braddock.

765
00:33:32,010 --> 00:33:34,838
What's going on?
I didn't killArcher!

766
00:33:34,925 --> 00:33:36,536
Then why were you
tryin' to steal his body?

767
00:33:36,623 --> 00:33:37,928
I'm not stealing him, either.

768
00:33:38,016 --> 00:33:40,540
I told you, I need
to perform a ritual

769
00:33:40,627 --> 00:33:44,413
to release the bad energy before
his journey to the afterlife.

770
00:33:44,500 --> 00:33:47,590
There was something inside
Braddock, wasn't there?

771
00:33:47,677 --> 00:33:49,244
Something that would
lead directly to you

772
00:33:49,331 --> 00:33:50,898
if the coroner
discovered it.

773
00:33:50,985 --> 00:33:52,639
You never did share
the proprietary blend

774
00:33:52,726 --> 00:33:55,511
of that stinky special
tea of yours, did you?

775
00:33:55,598 --> 00:33:57,252
The tea of clarity?

776
00:33:57,339 --> 00:33:58,906
It was killing him
quicker than the cancer.

777
00:33:58,993 --> 00:34:00,603
That's why
you stopped selling it.

778
00:34:00,690 --> 00:34:02,649
Oh, please. You're making
a terrible mistake.

779
00:34:02,736 --> 00:34:05,347
So you say, Brother Josef.

780
00:34:05,434 --> 00:34:08,176
At least let me
release his spirit.

781
00:34:08,263 --> 00:34:10,309
Let me do that for him, please.

782
00:34:10,396 --> 00:34:12,441
Let's go.
Hands behind your back.

783
00:34:18,969 --> 00:34:21,842
So the amount of radiation
released from a solar flare

784
00:34:21,929 --> 00:34:23,191
can damage satellites?

785
00:34:23,278 --> 00:34:24,932
It's way, way more powerful

786
00:34:25,019 --> 00:34:28,457
than the radiation therapy
used to treat cancer.

787
00:34:28,544 --> 00:34:29,676
Did you know this, Lisbon?

788
00:34:29,763 --> 00:34:31,460
- Fascinating.
- Now, listen.

789
00:34:31,547 --> 00:34:33,723
Aren't I allowed a-a, uh,
a phone call or something?

790
00:34:33,810 --> 00:34:34,768
In a minute.
Sit down.

791
00:34:34,855 --> 00:34:36,161
[cellphone ringing]

792
00:34:36,248 --> 00:34:38,119
Excuse me.

793
00:34:38,206 --> 00:34:40,295
Lisbon.

794
00:34:40,382 --> 00:34:41,340
Are you sure?

795
00:34:43,168 --> 00:34:46,562
No. No, we didn't take it.

796
00:34:46,649 --> 00:34:48,564
We'll be right there.
Get everybody out of that room.

797
00:34:48,651 --> 00:34:50,218
It's a crime scene now.

798
00:34:52,873 --> 00:34:55,441
Braddock's body is
missing from the morgue.

799
00:34:55,528 --> 00:34:57,921
They think somebody
stole it after we left.

800
00:34:58,008 --> 00:34:59,662
Oh, dear.

801
00:34:59,749 --> 00:35:01,316
He was right about one thing.

802
00:35:01,403 --> 00:35:04,102
We just made a terrible mistake.

803
00:35:04,189 --> 00:35:07,105
[instrumental music]

804
00:35:16,244 --> 00:35:19,117
No sign of the killer
or Braddock's body.

805
00:35:19,204 --> 00:35:21,206
Whoever took it won't make
the same mistake twice.

806
00:35:21,293 --> 00:35:23,164
They'll want to destroy
the body...quickly.

807
00:35:23,251 --> 00:35:24,600
How would you do
that in Agua Blanca?

808
00:35:24,687 --> 00:35:26,080
I can think of one way.

809
00:35:26,167 --> 00:35:28,343
Please tell me it doesn't
involve feeding tigers.

810
00:35:28,430 --> 00:35:29,953
Tigers leave bones.

811
00:35:30,040 --> 00:35:31,955
There is a more thorough option.

812
00:35:34,828 --> 00:35:37,787
[Lisbon]
'I hope
you're right about this.'

813
00:35:37,874 --> 00:35:39,572
Look at this clinic.

814
00:35:39,659 --> 00:35:41,835
Very well funded.

815
00:35:41,922 --> 00:35:43,967
Scheck's ego would require
that he has the best.

816
00:35:44,054 --> 00:35:45,708
'I tell you, this guy'

817
00:35:45,795 --> 00:35:48,146
is gonna have a top of
the line medical incinerator.

818
00:35:49,843 --> 00:35:51,714
In the basement?

819
00:35:51,801 --> 00:35:54,021
Can we use inside
voices, please?

820
00:35:57,067 --> 00:35:59,983
[instrumental music]

821
00:36:06,686 --> 00:36:07,687
Police!

822
00:36:07,774 --> 00:36:11,169
Drop the...weapon.

823
00:36:11,256 --> 00:36:12,909
Better late than never,
I suppose.

824
00:36:12,996 --> 00:36:16,086
Hands in the air!

825
00:36:16,174 --> 00:36:18,654
Your hands, not his.

826
00:36:18,741 --> 00:36:19,786
[Jane]
'Oh!'

827
00:36:24,617 --> 00:36:27,402
Did you act alone?

828
00:36:27,489 --> 00:36:29,839
Did Dr. Scheck have
anything to do with this?

829
00:36:29,926 --> 00:36:30,884
No.

830
00:36:30,971 --> 00:36:32,538
He'd never pull off..

831
00:36:35,628 --> 00:36:37,107
...never mind.

832
00:36:37,195 --> 00:36:40,763
It'll go a lot easier
if you just come clean.

833
00:36:42,243 --> 00:36:43,766
We looked at
your bank statements.

834
00:36:43,853 --> 00:36:46,900
You're very wealthy
for a nurse.

835
00:36:46,987 --> 00:36:50,251
Cancer survivors are often
grateful to their caregivers.

836
00:36:50,338 --> 00:36:52,471
You took bribes, didn't you?

837
00:36:54,690 --> 00:36:56,214
Cancer studies
are like lotteries

838
00:36:56,301 --> 00:36:59,391
and you were selling
the winning tickets.

839
00:36:59,478 --> 00:37:03,003
- You were selling hope.
- It's cheap at the price.

840
00:37:03,090 --> 00:37:05,788
Why were you trying to
destroy Archer Braddock's body?

841
00:37:07,268 --> 00:37:09,052
His liver was damaged.

842
00:37:09,923 --> 00:37:11,359
Alcoholism.

843
00:37:12,795 --> 00:37:13,970
And you missed it.

844
00:37:17,626 --> 00:37:20,281
Oh, we thought Brother Josef
was poisoning him with his tea

845
00:37:20,368 --> 00:37:23,806
when in fact
it was your drug.

846
00:37:23,893 --> 00:37:25,939
Because of the liver damage,
it was killing him

847
00:37:26,026 --> 00:37:27,767
faster than the cancer.

848
00:37:30,248 --> 00:37:32,206
I knew Dr. Scheck would find out

849
00:37:32,293 --> 00:37:34,252
in his postmortem exam.

850
00:37:36,079 --> 00:37:39,518
And then there would be
a whole investigation.

851
00:37:39,605 --> 00:37:42,521
And...they'd have
found out about..

852
00:37:42,608 --> 00:37:46,002
...about the gifts
I received from patients.

853
00:37:47,177 --> 00:37:48,440
I had to do something.

854
00:37:48,527 --> 00:37:50,180
So you went to
the banquet hall that night.

855
00:37:50,268 --> 00:37:51,617
You followed him there.

856
00:37:51,704 --> 00:37:54,097
He was gonna die anyway..

857
00:37:54,184 --> 00:37:55,882
...of bone cancer.

858
00:37:57,318 --> 00:38:00,147
Do you know
how agonizing that is?

859
00:38:00,234 --> 00:38:03,193
Excruciatingly slow and painful.

860
00:38:07,502 --> 00:38:09,374
[crackling]

861
00:38:09,461 --> 00:38:11,463
[Betsy]
I made his passing easy.

862
00:38:11,550 --> 00:38:14,161
A little chloroform
before I suffocated him

863
00:38:15,118 --> 00:38:16,990
he never felt a thing.

864
00:38:18,034 --> 00:38:20,123
I ended his suffering.

865
00:38:21,429 --> 00:38:23,388
It was an act
of kindness, really.

866
00:38:23,475 --> 00:38:26,869
I think Mr. Braddock and his
children would beg to differ.

867
00:38:31,918 --> 00:38:32,832
[Van Pelt]
'What you got there?'

868
00:38:32,919 --> 00:38:35,748
Huh.
It's my kid.

869
00:38:37,010 --> 00:38:39,360
- So big so fast.
- Mm.

870
00:38:39,447 --> 00:38:40,840
Only a couple weeks to go.

871
00:38:42,798 --> 00:38:46,628
I heard about today.
Are you okay?

872
00:38:46,715 --> 00:38:49,196
Yeah.

873
00:38:49,283 --> 00:38:50,719
You know,
when that guy shot at me

874
00:38:50,806 --> 00:38:52,068
all I could think about
was how I gotta make it out

875
00:38:52,155 --> 00:38:54,375
to see my son.

876
00:38:54,462 --> 00:38:56,334
It's crazy.

877
00:38:56,421 --> 00:38:58,379
You know, if I'd died a year ago

878
00:38:58,466 --> 00:38:59,380
it wouldn't have
mattered so much

879
00:38:59,467 --> 00:39:01,426
in the big scheme of things.

880
00:39:01,513 --> 00:39:04,864
- It would have mattered to me.
- Yeah.

881
00:39:04,951 --> 00:39:08,041
Not as much as it would
have mattered to him.

882
00:39:08,128 --> 00:39:10,086
He's a game changer.

883
00:39:14,308 --> 00:39:16,310
He's a lucky kid.

884
00:39:28,670 --> 00:39:30,280
Here we go.

885
00:39:30,368 --> 00:39:31,934
What's this for?

886
00:39:32,021 --> 00:39:35,024
Well, to let you know what
a proper cup of tea smells like

887
00:39:35,111 --> 00:39:36,591
before you go out
into the world.

888
00:39:38,463 --> 00:39:40,856
Brother Josef really did
a number on my dad, huh?

889
00:39:42,467 --> 00:39:43,642
Oh, I don't know.

890
00:39:43,729 --> 00:39:45,948
He gave him hope.

891
00:39:46,035 --> 00:39:47,297
No better way
to stimulate the body

892
00:39:47,385 --> 00:39:49,691
than the promise of a new day.

893
00:39:49,778 --> 00:39:53,434
And Helen?
I still can't believe that.

894
00:39:53,521 --> 00:39:55,654
I did a cancer walk
for that bitch.

895
00:39:55,741 --> 00:39:57,177
Language.

896
00:39:57,264 --> 00:39:59,919
Liesl?

897
00:40:00,006 --> 00:40:01,486
Aden?

898
00:40:04,489 --> 00:40:07,143
I-I talked to Aunt Marcia and..

899
00:40:07,230 --> 00:40:09,058
...I wanna move back home.

900
00:40:09,145 --> 00:40:11,583
- Okay.
- After rehab.

901
00:40:11,670 --> 00:40:14,107
Who talked you into that?

902
00:40:14,194 --> 00:40:15,413
Dad.

903
00:40:17,806 --> 00:40:20,113
[Archer Braddock]
Hey, kiddo, it's dad.
It's been a while.

904
00:40:20,200 --> 00:40:24,073
Look, I'm outside your job.
I made a mistake.

905
00:40:24,160 --> 00:40:26,075
I want you to come home.

906
00:40:26,162 --> 00:40:27,860
Whatever time I have left

907
00:40:27,947 --> 00:40:30,471
I wanna spend
with you and Liesl.

908
00:40:30,558 --> 00:40:32,473
I love you both so much.

909
00:40:32,560 --> 00:40:35,476
Okay. See you soon, I hope.

910
00:40:35,563 --> 00:40:37,391
Here goes.

911
00:40:37,478 --> 00:40:39,306
Come on, let's go home.

912
00:40:41,395 --> 00:40:42,962
Mr. Jane?

913
00:40:45,268 --> 00:40:46,574
Thanks.

914
00:40:52,406 --> 00:40:55,365
- Boss, you have a minute?
- Sure.

915
00:40:55,453 --> 00:40:58,847
I heard about you and Rigsby
with the drug dealer earlier.

916
00:40:58,934 --> 00:41:00,936
Nice work.

917
00:41:05,680 --> 00:41:07,073
Is everything alright?

918
00:41:07,160 --> 00:41:08,596
No.

919
00:41:10,903 --> 00:41:12,382
This is a safe room.

920
00:41:12,470 --> 00:41:14,907
You can talk about
whatever you'd like here.

921
00:41:16,865 --> 00:41:19,564
Okay.

922
00:41:19,651 --> 00:41:22,523
[instrumental music]

923
00:41:30,749 --> 00:41:33,926
I'm sorry,
I was late this morning.

924
00:41:34,013 --> 00:41:36,015
I'll make sure
it never happens again.

925
00:41:36,102 --> 00:41:37,364
Okay.

926
00:41:38,452 --> 00:41:40,585
Goodnight, boss.

927
00:41:40,672 --> 00:41:43,544
[instrumental music]

928
00:42:03,695 --> 00:42:06,567
[music continues]

929
00:42:19,362 --> 00:42:20,625
[toilet flushes]

930
00:42:33,246 --> 00:42:35,509
[theme music]

